**Note on scope.** The ROADMAP (Phase 3, decision 0057) names "OpenHands'
observation-masking condenser" as a source. At this commit the SDK's
`condenser/` package holds `NoOpCondenser`, `LLMSummarizingCondenser` and a
`PipelineCondenser`; a standalone observation-masking condenser is not present
in this history (superseded by the LLM-summarizing one, which 0057 explicitly
rejects as a compaction method because it rewrites). What was ported instead
is `utils.py`'s **token-budget suffix sizing**, the non-rewriting half of the
condenser package that decides how much of a transcript's tail survives a
token target -- the same problem 0057's truncation step solves.

Ported (Python to Rust) into crates/kgraf-session/src/reduction.rs: the
idea behind `get_shortest_prefix_above_token_count` /
`get_suffix_length_for_token_reduction` -- decide how much of the *oldest*
content must go to bring the total under a token target, keeping everything
newer. Upstream finds that cut point with a binary search over prefix length,
because its token count calls an LLM's tokenizer (an expensive call per
probe). kGraf's `Reducible::approx_tokens` is a local O(1) estimate, so
the port is a single linear scan over the oldest-first candidates instead of
a binary search over prefix length: same result (drop from the front until
under budget, keep the tail), cheaper for a cost function that is already
cheap. This is a deliberate change, not an unported detail; "from the tail"
describes what survives, not the direction of the scan itself.

Not ported from this file, added on top of it in `reduce`, with no upstream
equivalent found in this package:
- Entries sharing a `Reducible::truncation_unit` are dropped as one group or
  not at all (0057, and an owner review: a tool call and its result are one
  unit, so truncation must never orphan one half of a pair).
- `ReductionReport::over_budget` is set when pinned content alone (or pinned
  plus whatever truncation could not safely remove) still exceeds the target
  after both steps, rather than the caller silently receiving an
  over-the-budget view.
